    <!-- Intro -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        China produces more than half of the world's wire mesh, and the majority of it comes from one place: <strong>Anping County, Hebei Province</strong>—a cluster of 500+ factories making everything from stainless steel filtration mesh to galvanized welded mesh and gabion baskets. Importing directly from these factories can cut your costs by 30–50% compared to buying from domestic distributors, but only if you manage the process correctly.
      </p>
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        This guide walks you through the entire import process as a first-time buyer: from writing your specification to getting goods through customs. Follow the seven steps in order, and use the checklists below to avoid the mistakes that cost importers thousands of dollars. For background on why Anping dominates this industry, read our <a href="/blog/anping-wire-mesh-cluster/" class="text-theme-primary underline">Anping wire mesh cluster guide</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Step 1 -->
    <section id="step1" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Step 1: Define Your Specification Requirements</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        The single biggest cause of failed imports is an incomplete specification. Chinese factories quote exactly what you ask for—nothing more. If you write "stainless steel mesh, 100 mesh, 10 rolls," you may receive 201-grade stainless (a cheap, low-nickel alloy) with wire diameter 0.03 mm thinner than you expected. Vague specs invite substitutions.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Before contacting any supplier, write a one-page technical specification containing:
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Complete Specification Checklist</h3>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Product type (woven wire mesh, welded mesh, crimped mesh, dutch weave, etc.)</li>
            <li>☐ Material grade (e.g., 304, 316, 316L stainless; Q195 low-carbon steel for galvanized; Galfan wire)</li>
            <li>☐ Mesh count (wires per inch) or aperture/opening size in mm</li>
            <li>☐ Wire diameter in mm, with tolerance (±0.01 mm standard)</li>
            <li>☐ Weave type (plain, twill, reverse dutch, etc.)</li>
          </ul>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Roll or panel dimensions (width × length) and edge finish</li>
            <li>☐ Surface treatment (galvanized hot-dip vs electro; PVC coated; passivated)</li>
            <li>☐ Testing standard (ASTM E11 / ISO 9044 for woven mesh; ASTM A185 for welded)</li>
            <li>☐ Quantity, packaging (waterproof paper + woven bags is standard), and labeling</li>
            <li>☐ Certifications required (mill test report, SGS report, ISO 9001)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        If you are unsure about mesh count vs. micron rating, our <a href="/blog/wire-mesh-size-chart-explained/" class="text-theme-primary underline">wire mesh size chart explained</a> article covers the conversions, and the <a href="/tools/inquiry-template-generator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">inquiry template generator</a> will format your spec into a professional RFQ you can send to multiple factories. You should also estimate your total weight now—see <a href="/blog/how-to-calculate-wire-mesh-weight/" class="text-theme-primary underline">how to calculate wire mesh weight</a>—because freight quotes and the factory's minimum order both depend on it.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Pro Tip</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          Attach a photo or drawing of the mesh you need, even a rough one. Anping factories produce thousands of variants; a visual reference eliminates 90% of specification confusion.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Step 2 -->
    <section id="step2" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Step 2: Find Wire Mesh Factories in China</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        You have four main sourcing channels, each with different tradeoffs:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Channel</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Best For</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Watch Out For</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">B2B platforms (Alibaba, Made-in-China)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Comparing many quotes fast; Trade Assurance orders</td>
              <td class="p-3">Many "factories" are trading companies; inflated online prices</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Verified factory directories</td>
              <td class="p-3">Pre-screened real manufacturers; faster shortlisting</td>
              <td class="p-3">Smaller pool than open platforms</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Trade fairs (Canton Fair, Anping Wire Mesh Expo)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Meeting owners face-to-face; seeing samples</td>
              <td class="p-3">Travel cost; follow-up discipline required</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Referrals / sourcing agents</td>
              <td class="p-3">Complex custom products; QC in China</td>
              <td class="p-3">Agent fees 3–10%; agent may be tied to certain factories</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Wherever you search, prioritize <strong>actual manufacturers over trading companies</strong>. Manufacturers control quality directly and quote 5–15% lower. Start with our <a href="/factories/" class="text-theme-primary underline">verified Anping factory directory</a> to build your shortlist, then cross-check on Alibaba. Aim for 3–5 suppliers so you can compare quotes meaningfully.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Send each supplier the same RFQ document (the <a href="/tools/inquiry-template-generator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">inquiry template generator</a> produces one in under two minutes). Identical requests are the only way to get comparable quotes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Step 3 -->
    <section id="step3" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Step 3: Verify and Shortlist Suppliers</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Anyone can build a professional-looking Alibaba storefront. Verification separates real factories from resellers and fraudsters. Do all of the following before ordering samples:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Check the business license.</strong> Ask for the company's Chinese business license and verify the Unified Social Credit Code on the National Enterprise Credit Information system. Confirm "manufacturing" appears in the business scope.</li>
        <li><strong>Match the bank account name to the company name.</strong> If the beneficiary on the proforma invoice is an individual or a different company, walk away.</li>
        <li><strong>Video call the factory.</strong> Ask for a live video tour showing weaving machines, wire drawing equipment, and warehouse. A real factory has no problem with this.</li>
        <li><strong>Request mill certificates</strong> from recent production runs for your material grade.</li>
        <li><strong>Check export history.</strong> Ask for bill of lading samples (with client data redacted) showing shipments to your region.</li>
        <li><strong>Order a third-party audit</strong> ($150–400 via SGS, Bureau Veritas, or TÜV) for orders above $20,000. This is the gold standard of verification.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Red Flags at Verification Stage</h3>
        <ul class="text-theme-secondary space-y-2 text-sm">
          <li>🚩 Refuses video factory tour or third-party inspection</li>
          <li>🚩 Company registered less than 2 years ago with claims of "20 years experience"</li>
          <li>🚩 Quote 30%+ below every other supplier (material substitution is almost certain)</li>
          <li>🚩 Uses a free email domain (Gmail/QQ) on the proforma invoice instead of the corporate domain</li>
          <li>🚩 Pressure to pay the "deposit" to a personal account "to save fees"</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Step 4 -->
    <section id="step4" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Step 4: Order Samples and Test</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Never skip samples. For wire mesh, sample costs are low ($20–100 plus $30–80 courier via DHL/FedEx), and the information they provide is worth far more. Order from your top 2–3 shortlisted factories simultaneously.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        When samples arrive, test them against your specification:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Measure mesh count</strong> with a mesh counter gauge or by counting wires per inch under magnification</li>
        <li><strong>Measure wire diameter</strong> with a micrometer (0.01 mm resolution) at multiple points</li>
        <li><strong>Verify material grade</strong>—a magnet test is a rough screen (304/316 are essentially non-magnetic; 201 is more magnetic), but for confirmation use XRF testing at a local lab or university (~$50)</li>
        <li><strong>Check coating weight</strong> on galvanized mesh (zinc coating below spec is the most common cost-cutting trick)</li>
        <li><strong>Test in your actual application</strong>—run the mesh through your filter, machine, or installation process before approving mass production</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Approve samples in writing. State on the purchase order: "Mass production must match approved sample reference #X, retained by buyer." This gives you a contractual quality benchmark.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Step 5 -->
    <section id="step5" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Step 5: Negotiate Price and Incoterms</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Wire mesh pricing from Anping factories is genuinely negotiable, especially on orders above $5,000. Key levers:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Order volume:</strong> Asking for a price at 2× your real quantity, then negotiating down, reveals the real price curve. MOQs in Anping are flexible—factories often accept 1–5 rolls for standard mesh.</li>
        <li><strong>Payment terms:</strong> 30% T/T deposit + 70% against B/L copy is standard. Established relationships can move to 20/80 or open account terms.</li>
        <li><strong>Incoterm:</strong> FOB (Free On Board) is the best default for first-time importers—you control ocean freight and insurance. EXW saves the factory effort but leaves you managing Chinese inland transport and export clearance (hard without a forwarder in China). CIF looks convenient but hides freight markup and gives you no control over the shipping line.</li>
        <li><strong>Packaging:</strong> Waterproof paper inner + woven bag outer is usually included; request reinforced export pallets for panel products (worth the $30–80 upcharge).</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Always calculate your <strong>landed cost</strong>—product + inland transport + ocean freight + insurance + customs duty + port fees + delivery—before accepting a quote. Our <a href="/tools/fob-price-calculator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">FOB price calculator</a> breaks a factory quote into its true cost components so you can compare suppliers on equal footing. A quote that is 5% cheaper on FOB terms can be 15% more expensive once you add hidden local charges.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        See the <a href="#incoterms" class="text-theme-primary underline">Incoterms section below</a> for a full comparison of EXW, FOB, CFR, and CIF.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Step 6 -->
    <section id="step6" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Step 6: Payment, Contract, and Quality Control</h2>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3 mt-6">Payment Methods</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>T/T (telegraphic transfer):</strong> Standard. 30% deposit to start production, 70% after passing pre-shipment inspection, before B/L release. Bank fees: $20–50 per transfer.</li>
        <li><strong>Alibaba Trade Assurance:</strong> Adds platform escrow-style protection. Good for first orders under $10,000.</li>
        <li><strong>Letter of Credit (L/C):</strong> Bank-guaranteed payment. Use for orders above $50,000 or with new suppliers—but expect $300–800 in bank fees and strict document requirements.</li>
        <li><strong>Western Union / PayPal "friends and family" / crypto:</strong> Never. Irreversible payments are the hallmark of fraud.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3">The Purchase Contract</h3>
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Your proforma invoice becomes the contract once countersigned. It must state: full specification (from Step 1), quantity, unit price, total value, Incoterm and port, payment terms, production lead time, inspection rights, penalty clause for late delivery (typically 0.5–1% per week, capped at 10%), and the dispute jurisdiction. Keep product liability and quality wording tied to your approved sample.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3">In-Production and Pre-Shipment Inspection</h3>
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Do not pay the balance blind. For any order above $5,000, arrange an inspection:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>During production (DUPRO):</strong> Inspector visits mid-production to catch material or weave problems early. $100–200.</li>
        <li><strong>Pre-shipment inspection (PSI):</strong> Full check of finished goods before the balance payment. $150–300. Hire SGS, Bureau Veritas, QIMA, or a local Anping inspection agency.</li>
        <li><strong>DIY:</strong> Ask the factory for detailed photos/videos of the finished rolls with a tape measure and micrometer in frame, plus the packing list. Acceptable for small repeat orders only.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Step 7 -->
    <section id="step7" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Step 7: Shipping, Customs, and Delivery</h2>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3 mt-6">Choose Your Freight Mode</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Sea freight (FCL):</strong> A 20ft container holds roughly 25–28 tons of mesh; 40ft HQ about 26–28 tons (volume-limited for light mesh). Best value for orders above ~15 CBM. Transit 18–35 days to US/EU.</li>
        <li><strong>Sea freight (LCL):</strong> Share a container for small orders. Rates look cheap but local charges at destination ($80–200 per CBM) often double the cost—compare carefully.</li>
        <li><strong>Rail (China–Europe):</strong> 15–20 days to Duisburg/Hamburg/Madrid. Good middle ground on price and speed.</li>
        <li><strong>Air/express:</strong> Only for urgent samples or very high-value fine mesh. Mesh is heavy—air freight can exceed product cost.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3">Plan the Loading</h3>
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Wire mesh is dense. Rolled woven mesh loads efficiently, but panels, welded mesh sheets, and gabions eat container volume fast. Use the <a href="/tools/container-loading-calculator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">container loading calculator</a> to confirm your order actually fits—and tell the factory the loading plan so they pack to it. Overloading a 20ft container past 28 tons risks overweight fines at the port; confirm the container weight limit with your shipping line.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3">Documents You Will Receive</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Commercial Invoice</strong> and <strong>Packing List</strong> (used for customs valuation)</li>
        <li><strong>Bill of Lading (B/L)</strong> — the title to the goods; originals needed for release at destination unless "telex release" is arranged</li>
        <li><strong>Certificate of Origin</strong> (needed for preferential tariffs under some trade agreements)</li>
        <li><strong>Mill Test Certificate / SGS report</strong> (if contracted)</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3">Customs and Import Duty</h3>
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Wire mesh HS codes: woven stainless mesh typically falls under <strong>HS 7314.14 / 7314.19</strong>, welded mesh under <strong>HS 7314.20</strong>, and some fence products under <strong>HS 7314.30</strong>. Import duties vary by country—commonly 0–6% into the US, EU, and UK, plus VAT/GST. Confirm the correct HS code and duty rate with your customs broker before the ship sails, and check whether anti-dumping duties apply to your product in your market (some wire mesh categories have AD/CVD orders in the US). A licensed customs broker (usually $100–200 per entry) handles classification, filing, and duty payment.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Typical Total Timeline</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          Sourcing & verification: 1–2 weeks → Samples: 1–2 weeks → Production: 2–4 weeks → Inspection & booking: 1 week → Ocean transit: 3–5 weeks → Customs & delivery: 1 week. <strong>Plan for 8–10 weeks end-to-end</strong>, and order restocks before you run out.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Incoterms -->
    <section id="incoterms" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Incoterms Explained for Wire Mesh Buyers</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Incoterms define who pays for and manages each leg of the journey. For most first-time wire mesh importers, <strong>FOB Xingang (Tianjin)</strong> is the sweet spot—the factory handles production and delivery to the ship; you control the ocean freight, insurance, and destination costs.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Incoterm</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Factory Handles</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">You Handle</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Best For</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">EXW (Ex Works)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Goods ready at factory gate</td>
              <td class="p-3">All transport, export clearance, freight, import</td>
              <td class="p-3">Buyers with a forwarder/agent in China</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">FOB (Free On Board)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Production + inland delivery + export clearance, loaded on vessel</td>
              <td class="p-3">Ocean freight, insurance, import, delivery</td>
              <td class="p-3">✅ Most first-time importers</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">CFR (Cost & Freight)</td>
              <td class="p-3">FOB + ocean freight to your port</td>
              <td class="p-3">Insurance, import, delivery</td>
              <td class="p-3">Buyers who trust the freight quote</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">CIF (Cost, Insurance & Freight)</td>
              <td class="p-3">CFR + minimum insurance</td>
              <td class="p-3">Import, duties, delivery</td>
              <td class="p-3">Convenient, but check for hidden freight markup</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        One caution: factories quoting CIF often add $500–1,500 of freight markup versus booking the same space yourself. Get a parallel freight quote from a forwarder before accepting CIF. The <a href="/tools/fob-price-calculator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">FOB price calculator</a> helps you strip freight out of a CIF quote to compare apples to apples.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- QC Checklist -->
    <section id="qc-checklist" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Pre-Shipment Quality Control Checklist</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Hand this checklist to your inspector (or use it yourself with the factory's photos/videos):
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Inspection Checklist (per shipment)</h3>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Material grade matches contract (XRF spot-check on 1 roll per 10)</li>
            <li>☐ Mesh count within tolerance (±3% is typical; count 3 positions per roll)</li>
            <li>☐ Wire diameter within tolerance (micrometer, 5 measurements per roll)</li>
            <li>☐ Roll width and length within stated tolerance</li>
            <li>☐ Weave type correct (plain/twill/dutch) and free of weaving defects</li>
          </ul>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Zinc coating weight / surface treatment verified (coating thickness gauge)</li>
            <li>☐ Edge finish and selvage as specified</li>
            <li>☐ Quantity and roll numbers match packing list</li>
            <li>☐ Packaging: waterproof inner layer, no exposed metal, labels correct</li>
            <li>☐ Container loading: dry floor, dunnage as needed, photos of loading</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
        Agree on an AQL (Acceptable Quality Level) in the contract—AQL 2.5 for major defects and 4.0 for minor is standard for wire mesh. Without a stated AQL, "acceptable quality" is unenforceable.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Scams -->
    <section id="scams" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Common Scams and How to Avoid Them</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Wire mesh importing is generally safe when you verify properly, but these schemes catch inexperienced buyers every year:
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-4 mb-6">
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">1. Material Substitution</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            You order 304 stainless; you receive 201 or a 304/201 blend. 201 costs 40% less and rusts within months. <strong>Defense:</strong> require mill certificates, do XRF testing on samples and on the finished goods (via your PSI inspector), and write the exact grade and standard into the contract with a penalty clause.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">2. Wire Diameter Shaving</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            The factory weaves with wire 0.01–0.03 mm thinner than quoted. On a 10-ton order this "saves" them 5–8% of material cost while weakening your mesh. <strong>Defense:</strong> specify wire diameter with tolerance in the contract and micrometer-check every sampled roll at inspection.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">3. The Deposit Disappearance</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            A fake "factory" collects a 30–50% deposit and vanishes—or a real factory's email gets hacked and the hacker sends new bank details. <strong>Defense:</strong> verify bank beneficiary = company name, confirm any bank detail change by phone with a known contact, use Trade Assurance or escrow for first orders, and never pay more than 30% upfront.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">4. The Trading Company Masquerade</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            A reseller presents itself as a factory, marks up the price, and—worse—you lose direct quality control. <strong>Defense:</strong> check the business license for manufacturing scope, insist on the video factory tour, and confirm the production address matches the registered address.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">5. Lowball Quote, Then "Cost Adjustments"</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            The quote is unrealistically cheap; after you pay the deposit, "material prices increased" or "packaging was not included" appears. <strong>Defense:</strong> fixed-price contract language—"Total price is final and includes all packaging, export clearance, and FOB charges"—and 3–5 comparable quotes so you know the market rate.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">6. Short Shipment</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            You pay for 100 rolls; the container holds 93. <strong>Defense:</strong> your inspector counts rolls against the packing list before the balance payment, and the contract states quantity tolerance (e.g., ±0% on count; weight verified on a weighbridge ticket).
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How much does it cost to import wire mesh from China?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Product cost depends on type: galvanized welded mesh runs $0.30–0.80/m², stainless woven mesh $2–15/m² depending on mesh count and alloy. Add inland + ocean freight ($1,000–3,000 for a 20ft container to US/EU), insurance (~0.3% of value), import duty (0–6% in most markets, plus VAT/GST), and broker fees ($100–200). Use the <a href="/tools/fob-price-calculator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">FOB price calculator</a> to model your landed cost before committing.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What is the minimum order quantity (MOQ) when importing wire mesh?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Anping factories are flexible. Standard woven or welded mesh: often 1–5 rolls (30–100 m each) or one pallet. Custom specifications (special alloy, unusual aperture): 100–500 m is typical. Very large or heavy welded panels may be quoted by weight (1–3 tons minimum). Trading companies and Alibaba resellers often impose higher MOQs than direct factories.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Do I need an import license or customs bond to import wire mesh?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Most wire mesh requires no import license in the US, EU, UK, or Australia. In the US, you need a customs bond for commercial entries over $2,500 (continuous bond ~$400–500/year). In the EU you need an EORI number (free, applied online). Always confirm the correct HS code—7314.14/7314.19 for woven mesh, 7314.20 for welded—and check for anti-dumping duties on your specific product in your market.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How long does it take to import wire mesh from China?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            End-to-end, plan 8–10 weeks: 1–2 weeks sourcing and verification, 1–2 weeks samples, 2–4 weeks production, 1 week inspection and booking, 3–5 weeks ocean transit (18–35 days depending on route), and about 1 week for customs clearance and final delivery. Rail to Europe takes 15–20 days transit instead of 30–40 by sea.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Should I use a sourcing agent or buy directly from the factory?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Buy directly if you can handle communication, inspection, and logistics yourself—agents add 3–10% commission. Use an agent if your order combines products from multiple factories, you need in-China QC, or language/timezone barriers are blocking progress. Either way, verify the actual manufacturer as described in Step 3. Our <a href="/factories/" class="text-theme-primary underline">factory directory</a> lets you shortlist verified manufacturers without paying agent fees.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Is Alibaba Trade Assurance enough protection for a first order?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            It is good but not complete. Trade Assurance covers you if goods don't ship or clearly don't match the contract, but claims require documentation and take weeks. Combine it with the fundamentals: verified company, samples approved, 30/70 payment split, and a pre-shipment inspection before the balance payment. For orders above $20,000, add an independent third-party inspection regardless of Trade Assurance.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How do I know if a Chinese wire mesh supplier is a factory or a trading company?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Ask for the business license and check the business scope for manufacturing (生产/制造). Cross-check the registered address with the production address shown in a live video tour. Factories usually answer technical weaving questions instantly and can show machines running your product; trading companies hesitate or need to "check with the engineer." Directories like our <a href="/factories/" class="text-theme-primary underline">Anping factory directory</a> pre-screen for real manufacturers.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

